WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.730
Ground units, gates are open

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:07.270
On January 20, 2012, police raided a mansion
in New Zealand at the request of the FBI.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.890
Confirm located target.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.890
Roger.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:13.610
Judging from the military-style raid, you’d
think they were after a terrorist or some

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:14.940
other dangerous criminal.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.570
But the man renting this mansion north of
Auckland was an internet entrepreneur who

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.210
ran one of the most popular websites in the
world.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:27.470
Megaupload was the 13th most frequently visited
site on the Internet at its peak.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:30.470
It claimed 50 million daily visits, accounting
for 4% of the total traffic on the Internet.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:35.149
Users could view any files people shared - movies,
music, and TV shows.

00:00:35.149 --> 00:00:37.499
Hello everyone, this is Kim Dotcom

00:00:37.499 --> 00:00:44.199
Kim Dotcom, also known as Kim Schmitz or Kimble
or Kim Tim Jim Vestor created Megaupload in

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:45.199
2005.

00:00:45.199 --> 00:00:47.309
He is larger than life.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:50.940
Standing at 6’7” and over 300 pounds.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.109
As a successful entrepreneur in his native
Germany, he had a knack for finding financial

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:57.160
opportunities in technology.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:02.019
He realized back in 2005 that there wasn’t
an easy way to send big files to family and

00:01:02.019 --> 00:01:03.019
friends.

00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:04.019
So, he created Megaupload.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:09.510
If a file was too big to share by email, you
could upload it to megaupload.com, and then

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.799
share a unique link so your family and friends
could access the file from the cloud.

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:16.850
People did use Megaupload for legitimate purposes.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:23.300
However, Megaupload made it very easy to share
large video files like movies and TV shows.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.190
Authorities believe THAT was the main money
maker - sharing pirated content.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:31.469
It wasn’t immediately obvious there was
pirated content available as there was no

00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:36.770
search feature on the site so you couldn’t
specifically look for pirated movies and shows.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.649
Instead, Megaupload relied on third-party
sites to publish URLs.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:46.060
For example, if you visited alluc.org, it
would contain a megaupload.com link.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:51.049
Or when an FBI agent investigating the company
visited sidereel.com to search for an episode

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:57.289
of Modern Family, they found 60 URLs hosted
on Megaupload’s sister site, Megavideo.com

00:01:57.289 --> 00:01:58.740
which streams video.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.880
Although copyright holders could submit a
claim to try to get the material taken down,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.010
the U.S. government said the takedown request
only removed the URL link in question, not

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:13.660
the actual copyrighted content, and since
multiple URLs pointed to the same file, the

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.890
content would still be available through other
undisclosed URL links.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.170
Hollywood’s major film studios were livid.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:26.080
The FBI accused the Mega brand of costing
film studios and record companies more than

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.940
$500 million.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.130
Perhaps in a bid to get some support on his
side, Kim Dotcom released a bizarre music

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:36.340
video with recording artists and celebrities
praising Megaupload.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:41.910
When I gotta send files across the globe,
I use Megaupload.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.430
Mega mega upload.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:48.240
But apparently, that wasn’t enough to appease
Hollywood.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.520
Authorities moved in on Megaupload though
Washington has denied it bowed to pressure.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.970
Mr Dotcom has been shown the warrant to search
the property

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:01.770
They timed the massive raid to coincide with
Dotcom’s 38th birthday celebration which

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.670
they expected would be attended by other Mega
executives.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:10.670
They also happened to make the move on the
same day U.S. lawmakers axed anti-piracy legislation

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.400
after heavy criticism from the public.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.000
They found Kim Dotcom sheltered in his panic
room

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.900
inside his mansion with a shotgun.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.050
They arrested him and as well three other
executives during the raid.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:26.740
Chief Marketing Officer Finn Batato,
Lead Programmer Bram van der Kolk,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.170
Chief Technical Officer Matthias Ortmann.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:34.110
They were charged with: racketeering, copyright
infringement, money laundering, and criminal

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.290
copyright infringement.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.100
If the FBI had their way, the men
would be extradited to America

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.130
each to face up to 55 years in prison.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.460
Megaupload was officially shut down.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:49.920
Usually, copyright infringement is a civil
matter and does NOT constitute a criminal

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.720
offense in the U.S.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.330
Dotcom’s lawyers argued that copyright infringement
is NOT a criminal offense in New Zealand

00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:00.710
where he was residing at the time,
and therefore, not an extraditable offense.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:05.210
And besides, Dotcom wrote in a blog post:
“...how could we be criminally responsible

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:06.740
for the actions of others?”

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:10.050
As in users who chose to upload pirated material.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:14.550
Apple co-founder Steve Wozniak made the same
argument, pointing out you can’t shut down

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:19.460
the Post Office if someone sends drugs through
the mail and he saw Megaupload as the post

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.460
office.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.790
But the U.S. government saw it differently.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:28.250
Washington believed Megaupload actively encouraged
users to post copyrighted content and SHOULD

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.760
be held criminally responsible.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.140
The Department of Justice wrote in this 191-page
evidence report that Megaupload’s business

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:39.030
model incentivized users to upload popular
pirated content.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:44.090
They described how they had a rewards program
that “offered to pay up to USD $10,000 to

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.660
users whose files were downloaded 5,000,000
times.”

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.110
They say Megaupload executives knew EXACTLY
what they were doing.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.690
Citing a Skype conversation in which the chief
programmer said to the chief tech officer:

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:57.640
“we’re modern pirates :-)”

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.770
The Mega group apparently raked in a treasure
trove.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:07.510
The company is said to have earned $175 million
- $25 million from ads and $150 million from

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:11.410
Premium memberships which offered faster,
unlimited service.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.070
Dotcom wasn’t shy about flaunting his lavish
lifestyle.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:19.470
In this music video posted to his YouTube
channel, he showed off his superyacht, luxury

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.389
cars, speed boats, and private jet.

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:26.400
During the raid, authorities seized millions
of dollars worth of assets - including his

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.260
cars and artwork.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.639
Dotcom felt the raid was excessive and blamed
it on the “subservience of New Zealand to

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:37.180
the US Empire and the corruption at the top
of government”.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:41.920
Turns out, New Zealand’s intelligence agency
illegally spied on Dotcom leading up to the

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:42.920
raid.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.110
The then prime minister John Key had to publicly
apologize.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:51.170
The injustice Dotcom felt he faced prompted
him to set up a new political organization

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.360
in New Zealand, the Internet Party.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.210
However, it failed to win any seats in the
New Zealand House of Representatives during

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:02.080
the 2017 general election and hasn’t contested
any elections since.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.890
Ten years after the raid, in May 2022, two
executives pleaded guilty to their involvement

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:12.570
in running Megaupload as part of a deal to
avoid extradition to the US.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:17.240
If Ortmann and van der Kolk are found guilty,
they face a maximum of ten years in prison

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.139
in New Zealand – which is far less than
what they could have faced in U.S. prison.

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:26.310
American prosecutors dropped their bid to
extradite the fourth executive Finn Batato

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:29.020
after he was diagnosed with cancer.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.020
Batato suffered greatly because of the whole
ordeal.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:35.560
He had flown in from Germany for Dotcom’s
birthday bash when he was arrested during

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.900
the raid and wasn’t allowed to go home.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:40.150
He was stuck in New Zealand.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:46.210
Dotcom says Batato began drinking every day
to drown out his sadness and it made him sick.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:50.390
During his final years, he started a family
in New Zealand but wouldn’t ever see his

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:52.639
three young children grow up.

00:06:52.639 --> 00:06:57.840
He passed away in June at the age of 49.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.000
As for his colleagues, Ortmann and van der
Kolk say they wanted to put the decade-long

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:08.000
legal saga behind them, and are now contributing
to society through Mega, a legitimate cloud-storage

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.170
site they set up after their arrest.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:14.940
Dotcom’s two former colleagues are expected
to testify against him as part of the deal,

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.950
but he doesn’t appear to hold any grudges,
tweeting:

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:21.639
After 10 years of US lawfare I understand
why they have given up.

00:07:21.639 --> 00:07:26.500
I don’t blame them and I sincerely wish
them all the best.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:33.090
As for himself, Dotcom is out on bail in New
Zealand as he fights extradition to the US.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:37.350
He frequently posts updates on social media
on life with his children and his second wife

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:43.340
Elizabeth Donnelly whom he married on the
anniversary of the raid, on January 20, 2018.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:48.930
How this saga plays out could be an important
test of just how far the Americans can reach

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.700
when it comes to intellectual property rights.

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:56.940
Kim Dotcom has been cast as a visionary, online
freedom campaigner, or, if the U.S. government

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.370
and Hollywood are to be believed, a swindler
and a thief.

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:01.370
Which do you think he is?

